== 1...b6 · Owen's defence ==

A very rare and uncommon response to the King's pawn game It may transpose to an English opening or other lines.

It is offbeat in that Black allows White to play [[/2. d4|'''2. d4''']] and easily control the centre. Black usually fianchettos the light-squared bishop to b7, and may plan to assault White's big centre with ...c5.

=== History ===
1...b6 is named after [[w:John Owen (chess player)|John Owen]] (1827―1901), an English vicar and chess master, who scored a surprise victory with it over [[w:Paul Morphy|Paul Morphy]] in 1858.<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1266597 Morphy v Owen, 1858. Chessgames.com]</ref> 

[[w:Gioachino Greco|Gioachino Greco]], 17th century chess author, published the earliest games in this line. He published two miniatures, one that gave a refutation to 1. e4 b6 2. d4 Bb7 3. Bd3 f5? and another where Black fianchettos both bishops.<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1243022 Greco v NN, 1623. Chessgames.com]</ref><ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1271590 Greco v NN, 1623. Chessgames.com]</ref>

Owen's defence is sometimes seen among club players.

