== 2...Qxd5 · Classical variation ==

Black retakes the pawn immediately. 

[[/3. Nc3|'''3. Nc3''']] is White's usual continuation.<ref>90% of games in Lichess's masters database, 76% of games in Lichess's player database.</ref> White develops a knight tempo on the queen, and Black must pick a safe square to where their queen will be safe: typically Qa5 or Qd8. 

[[/3. Nf3|'''3. Nf3''']] is also tried. White's idea is to delay playing Nc3, realising that they may pick up the tempo on Black's queen later. Without playing Nc3 yet, White has the idea of taking over the queenside by putting both c and d pawns in the centre (Nc3 would obstruct the c pawn's way to c4). 3...Bg4 is the typical move, then 4. Be2 Nc6 5. d4 O-O-O. 

[[/3. d4|'''3. d4''']] first is similar to the 3. Nf3 lines, transposing after 3...Nc6 4. Nf3 Bg4 5. Be2 O-O-O. White keeps the option of c4.

[[/3. c4|'''3. c4?''']] immediately may look like it kicks Black's queen and allows White to develop Nc3 only after getting the c-pawn out of the way--however 3...Qe4+ wins Black a pawn (4. Ne2 Qxc4, or 4. Be2 Qxg2) or a favourable queen trade. 

On the subject of bad moves, the worst possible move here is [[/3. Ke2|'''3. Ke2??''']], leading to a three turn checkmate (3...Qe4#). 

=== History ===

Recapturing the pawn immediately with the queen is the classical approach and was the line used in chess game recorded in the 15th century poem [[wikipedia:Sachs d'amor|''Scachs d'amor'']]. 

Several online opening books give this line the name '''Mieses-Kotrč variation'''.<ref>[https://www.chess.com/openings/Scandinavian-Defense-Mieses-Kotrc-Variation ''Scandinavian Defense: Mieses-Kotrč Variation'']. Chess.com</ref><ref>[https://lichess.org/opening/Scandinavian_Defense_Mieses-Kotroc_Variation ''Mieses-Kotroc Variation'']. Lichess.org</ref><ref>[https://chesstempo.com/game-database/opening/scandinavian-defense-mieses-kotroc-variation/1908 ''Scandinavian Defense, Mieses-Kotroc Variation'']. Chesstempo.com</ref> Though [[w:Jacques Mieses|Jacques Mieses]] (1865–1954) was an expert on the Scandinavian defence and published analysis on it, his association with 2...Qxd5 has only come about recently and may stem from an ECO code misunderstanding. Both 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 and the "Mieses-Kotrč gambit"<ref>or Kotrč-Mieses gambit. NB: Itself may be a misnomer: the gambit was played ''against'' Mieses by Leonhardt, and Kotrč has no clear association.</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=The Oxford Companion to Chess |last=Hooper |first=David |publisher=Oxford University Press |year=1984 |location=Oxford |pages=173 |last2=Wyld |first2=Kenneth}}</ref> (an alternate name for the [[Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...d5/2. exd5/2...Qxd5/3. Nc3/3...Qa5/4. b4|Leonhardt gambit, 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qa5 4. b4?!]]) appear under the ECO code B01. 

