== 1. h3 · Clemenz opening ==

This is an uncommon, passive opening move which achieves nothing in terms of development or control of the centre. 

At best 1. h3 is a waiting move that forgoes White's first turn advantage, but otherwise is not a serious error. e.g. after [[/1...e5|'''1...e5''']] 2. e4 it is as if White is playing the Black side of the open game, where the addition of h3 controls g4 and prevents ...Bg4. Other "reversed" lines are possible: 2. c4 reaches a position from the English opening or "reversed Sicilian" ([[w:Michael Basman|Michael Basman]] liked this approach, and to continue with an early g4);<ref>{{Cite book |title=Unorthodox Chess Openings |last=Schiller |first=Eric |publisher=Cardoza Publishing |year=1998 |location=New York |pages=94-6}}</ref> [[/1...d5|'''1...d5''']] 2. d4 reaches a reversed closed game, etc. 

However, White often plays 1. h3 with a more unorthodox idea in mind. 1...e5 2. a3? d4 reaches [[w:Michael Basman|Michael Basman]]'s<ref>{{Cite book |title=Unorthodox Chess Openings |last=Schiller |first=Eric |publisher=Cardoza Publishing |year=1998 |location=New York |pages=97}}</ref> "[[Chess Opening Theory/1. a3/1...e5/2. h3/2...d5|creepy-crawly]]" opening.

[[/1...h5|'''1...h5''']] fails to punish White's lack of initiative, weakening Black's kingside. It resembles the [[Chess Opening Theory/1. h4|Desprez opening]] for Black, with a possible idea of opening up the black rook. It is notable only insofar as the line 1. h3 h5 2. g4?? has attracted a name, the Spike Lee gambit.  

=== History ===
An early recorded game in this opening was played in 1857 between Charles Mead (1815―1876), the President of the American Chess Association, and [[w:Paul Morphy|Paul Morphy]] (1837―1884). Morphy, with the black pieces, trounced Mead.<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1336490 Mead v Morphy, 1857. Chessgames.com]</ref> 

The opening is named for [[w:Hermann Clemenz|Hermann Clemenz]] (1846―1908), an Estonian chess master.<ref>{{Cite book |title=The Oxford Companion to Chess |last=Hooper |first=David |publisher=Oxford University Press |year=1984 |location=Oxford |pages=68 |last2=Wyld |first2=Kenneth}}</ref> In modern times the opening is most associated with offbeat chess aficionado IM Michael Basman.

